# Review package: batch 7, slice relay

## 8c2090d Refuse new connections to a blocked or freed name at once, without a restart

frps now asks relay.py about every new connection to an https or stcp proxy (R7.4). A name that is blocked or freed is
refused at once, with one read of bindings by its primary key: no write, no dashboard read, no lock. The owner's key is
not looked at, so a tunnel keeps taking connections after a key change (R6.1). The edge passes on its token alone. The
visitor's address is neither logged nor kept. `admin ban <name> [reason] --cut` ends with `restart frps`, which
hornhoof-relay acts on, after Caddy's list.

Review Focus 5 turned up two waits that no trace line can show, since a line's ms starts once the request is read and
ends before its answer goes out. On a kept connection each answer took about 44 ms, Nagle's rule holding its body for the
delayed ACK of its headers; it is 0.35 ms now. A crowd at once overflowed the relay's queue of 5 connections
(ListenOverflows +161 to +220 per 200 at once, 0 now), which cost its visitors the kernel's retry. Both have clock-free
tests.

test_the_droplet_logs_no_visitor_address now looks for both forms of the visitor's port. At the droplet's log levels it
finds neither, on all four hosts, in frps, the edge, Caddy or relay.py, and no leak beyond what the README says. A new test
pins that Caddy's 502 line does carry the form these checks look for. A refused visitor leaves no address either.

Proven by: 28 tests of test_block_by_name, and each wrong version tried (a write, a lock, a dashboard read, a table scan,
a sleep, the address in the trace, the owner's key, the edge by the link rule) turned its test red. The two crowd tests
passed 6 of 6 runs under coverage on one core shared with three spinners.

Ruling: test_new_user_conn_burst allows 10 of 200 trace lines over 50 ms, not none - why: a wall clock on a loaded machine; one preemption gave 29.5 ms on a shared core, a stall lands only on the calls in flight, and what relay.py itself could make wait is pinned without a clock in NoWaitTests and WaitsNoTraceLineShowsTests - cost if wrong: a slow path that hits under 5% of calls and is none of a write, lock, dashboard read or scan goes unseen by this test.
Ruling: Handler sets TCP_NODELAY and RelayServer's queue is 1024, neither in the plan - why: Review Focus 5's own promise, measured above; the plan's trace cannot see either - cost if wrong: none found; loopback only, and the kernel caps the queue at somaxconn.
Ruling: test_a_crowd_at_once_is_refused_nowhere added, and the fake company's queue is 512 - why: the plan paces 200 over 2 s but Review Focus 5 says at once; 9 of 200 failed on the fake's queue of 5, not on the relay - cost if wrong: none. At once, no timing is asserted: fresh threads each open SQLite, p95 about 5 ms, worst 46 to 64 ms seen.
Ruling: a proxy named edge without the token is refused outright, never by the link rule - why: fail closed; edge is reserved, so no company can hold it - cost if wrong: none.
Ruling: a service's link is CLIENT applied to <name without its service>-peer - why: CLIENT needs a kind, so the plan's wording cannot match as written - cost if wrong: none; its table test pins hyphens and a link named data-app.
Ruling: the trace line keeps run_id and client beside the plan's op, proxy_name, ms and reject - why: one shape for every op, and neither names a visitor - cost if wrong: none.
Ruling: admin() split into small functions, behaviour unchanged - why: its CRAP was over 10 and the ban changes it - cost if wrong: none; the old tests of its commands pass.
Ruling: Stack.start_relay wraps _start_relay - why: an old test calls the private name, and old tests are not rewritten - cost if wrong: none.

